00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,000
Today. I'm talking with Dr Paul Auerbach,

139
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:13,000
and he is a Professor of Surgery in the Division of Emergency Medicine

140
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:15,000
at Stanford University.

141
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
He's the world's leading authority on wilderness medicine

142
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
and is one of the world's leading authorities on emergency medicine,

143
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
and he's the author of Medicine for the Outdoors,

144
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:26,000
which is now in its fifth edition.

145
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
He's been involved in the Haiti earthquake

146
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:33,000
and he's been involved in the Haiti earthquake

147
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:36,000
and he's been involved in the Haiti earthquake disaster response.

148
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
He's acted as a volunteer physician in Guatemala

149
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:44,000
and is working with the Nepal ambulance service in Kathmandu, Nepal.

150
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
Dr Auerbach is certainly a champion of giving back

151
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:49,000
and trying to make the world a better place,

152
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
and today he joins us from his office there at Stanford.

153
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:54,000
Good to have you, Dr.

154
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:56,000
Thank you for inviting me.

155
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:58,000
We're talking to scout leaders today.

156
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
I know a lot of us have read Medicine for the Outdoors

157
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:04,000
and I have the current copy and I keep it close by.

158
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:06,000
Do you have any scouting background?

159
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:08,000
I do.

160
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:14,000
I was a sea scout for a couple of years growing up in New Jersey.

161
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:16,000
Oh, okay,

162
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:18,000
Prior to that I was a Cub Scout.

163
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
I never went on into boy scouting.

164
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:25,000
I took a bit of a break for athletics and other things

165
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:29,000
and then rejoined scouting as a sea scout

166
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
because it seemed novel and interesting

167
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:35,000
and I had an interest in the ocean and marine science.

168
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:37,000
Uh-huh, Whereabouts in New Jersey,

169
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
Central Jersey, a place called North Plainfield?

170
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:44,000
Oh sure, Yeah, I'm very close by there actually.

171
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:48,000
Well, I can talk like that and you can call me Polly if you want to.

172
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:50,000
Okay,

173
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
Wilderness medicine- it's kind of a new discipline.

174
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,000
What got you interested in wilderness medicine?

175
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
It's new, but it's not so new actually,

176
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
If you think about it historically.

177
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
probably medicine at the beginning of time

178
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
was what we could consider to be wilderness medicine

179
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,000
because it was practiced in austere circumstances

180
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:12,000
where people had limited resources.

181
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:17,000
Modern wilderness medicine, congealed as a specialty

182
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,000
over the last 30 years, I would say,

183
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:25,000
beginning particularly with folks interested in mountaineering

184
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,000
high altitude, and then amalgamating that

185
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:34,000
with special interests such as dive medicine, military medicine,

186
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:39,000
what people needed to do to survive and prosper in other environments

187
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:43,000
such as the deserts, the forests, et cetera.

188
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:49,000
My interest in it as a specialty began when I served as an extern

189
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
on an Indian reservation in Montana

190
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:55,000
during the summer of my second year as a medical student.

191
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:59,000
Out there I was asked to help treat victims

192
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
who had been bitten by snakes or near drowned

193
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
or struck by lightning or had environmental exposures,

194
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,000
and I discovered that while there was some literature

195
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:10,000
about these topics, it was pretty scattered.

196
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:14,000
So that put the first seeds in my mind

197
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:18,000
for perhaps creating what has become the textbook

198
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:20,000
entitled Wilderness Medicine,

199
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
which is about to go into its sixth edition

200
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
and is written for medical professionals.

201
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:27,000
How long ago are we talking?

202
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,000
That was in 1975.

203
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:32,000
I would imagine that a lot of the protocols

204
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
and the things that have developed since then

205
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:39,000
are pretty big steps forward in what you had to work with at the time.

206
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
Wilderness medicine is like any other aspect of medicine,

207
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:44,000
I think. we run in cycles

208
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:48,000
and a lot of what we were taught three decades ago

209
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:52,000
has been reevaluated and we see changes constantly.

210
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
So, whether it's treatment for snake bite

211
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:57,000
or management of hypothermia,

212
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:02,000
we continually learn new and better ways to manage patients.

213
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
But, like all of medicine, there are some things that don't change.

214
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:09,000
So the procedures whereby we splint fractures

215
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
and manage certain types of wounds,

216
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
the need for hydration in certain circumstances-

217
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:19,000
One of the most common things that we encounter with scouts

218
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:21,000
is the whole hydration thing.

219
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:23,000
Maybe you can give us kind of an encapsulation

220
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:25,000
on what best practices are at this point.

221
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:29,000
Hydration is actually not all that complicated.

222
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
The real issue is recognizing dehydration

223
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:34,000
and then modifying behavior

224
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:36,000
so that people will keep up with their hydration

225
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
and not only ingest enough water,

226
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:43,000
but ingest enough of the electrolytes-

227
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:46,000
sodium, potassium as examples- that they need

228
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:50,000
for all the systems that they have in their bodies to function well.

229
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
It's relevant in every single environment:

230
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
In the cold, at altitude and in the heat.

231
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:00,000
we know that thirst is not an adequate indicator for hydration,

232
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
so you have to pay attention to the volumes that you produce.

233
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:09,000
Certainly, we're seeing the life-saving efforts at hydration

234
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:11,000
that are occurring now in Haiti

235
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
with the cholera epidemic.

236
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:17,000
Anyone who's a diver knows that when they're under pressure

237
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
and in the cold that they urinate more

238
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:22,000
and so have to pay attention to hydration.

239
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:24,000
Can you over hydrate

240
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:26,000
Absolutely?

241
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
For instance, if you are losing large volumes of water

242
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:33,000
at times of high sweating or evaporative heat loss

243
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:37,000
and you only replenish that with water and no electrolytes,

244
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:41,000
then you can drive down the amount of sodium that's in your body

245
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:43,000
and make yourself quite ill.

246
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
There are definitely techniques and rules

247
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
for keeping up with oral hydration,

248
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:55,000
and that's why there exist products such as oral rehydration salts,

249
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:59,000
Gatorade and all the other commercial sports beverages

250
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:01,000
and electrolyte beverages.

251
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:03,000
There's a wonderful new product on the market,

252
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
By the way, I have no commercial interest in this,

253
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
but I'm seeing things that are out there called ELITE,

254
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:13,000
E-L-E-T-E, which are concentrated electrolyte drops,

255
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
such that you can replenish electrolytes

256
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:22,000
by putting these completely tasteless drops into water

257
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:26,000
and thereby restore essential electrolytes to your body.

258
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:28,000
These were very important.

259
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:32,000
We used them constantly when we deployed to Haiti a year ago.

260
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:34,000
It's one of the more common things.

261
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:37,000
It's highlighted frequently in athletics

262
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:39,000
and we have plenty of tragedies,

263
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:46,000
such as football players that succumb to exhaustion and dehydration

264
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:51,000
because they haven't adequately rehydrated during practices.

265
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
Military has a huge issue with it

266
00:17:55,000 --> 00:18:00,000
because they're deploying into climates in full clothing and regalia

267
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:02,000
and they need to keep the troops hydrated.

268
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:05,000
Staying well hydrated is a good rule for life in general.

269
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:09,000
What's the difference between first aid and wilderness medicine?

270
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:14,000
First aid is a subset of wilderness medicine and medicine in general.

271
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:18,000
First aid is just what it says:

272
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,000
It's the first encounter with the patient

273
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
and the aid that's rendered. at the initial encounter

274
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:30,000
You do what you can acutely to hold off the situation

275
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
or improve the situation, not have it worsen-

276
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:38,000
and then, if necessary, get the victim to more advanced care

277
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:40,000
if that's necessary.

278
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:42,000
Sometimes first aid is sufficient.

279
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:45,000
Cleaning a minor wound and putting antiseptic ointment

280
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:47,000
and a band-aid on it is first aid and that's sufficient aid

281
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:49,000
That takes you all the way through it.

282
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
But if someone has, for instance, a severe wrist fracture,

283
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,000
first aid might be splitting the fracture,

284
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
giving some pain medications, fashioning a sling, elevating it

285
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:01,000
and then bringing the victim to advanced care

286
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:06,000
where an orthopedist can reduce the fracture if necessary,

287
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:10,000
operate if necessary and provide the additional care that the victim needs.

288
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:17,000
Have you tracked any trends in outdoor or wilderness-related activity injuries

289
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:19,000
that you could advise us about?

290
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:23,000
There isn't a new wave of a particular type of injuries

291
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:25,000
that we're seeing associated with outdoor activities,

292
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:29,000
but I think we have better epidemiology to let us know

293
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:31,000
what the common injuries and illnesses are

294
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:36,000
and thereby better educate both the treatment of them

295
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:38,000
and, even more important, the prevention of them.

296
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
Is there a common set of preventative measures

297
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:45,000
that people who are leading groups in these activities should be aware of?

298
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
Well, I'm going to use the Boy Scout model. I'd be prepared.

299
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:52,000
The most important thing is actually to know what you're going to encounter,

300
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:55,000
to carry the appropriate equipment and to be trained ahead of time

301
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:57,000
how you're going to manage it.

302
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:01,000
So, as much as I'm a fan of my own book,

303
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:05,000
I don't want you to have to read about snake bites

304
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:10,000
and have your first educational encounter with the concept

305
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
in the middle of the woods after someone's been bitten by a rattlesnake.

306
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:17,000
I would far prefer that you have read something about it,

307
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:19,000
have some familiarity with splinting

308
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:23,000
and what you'll do in the event that the accident occurs.

309
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:25,000
So preparation is enormous.

310
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:30,000
I'm a big believer in using reasonable safety devices

311
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:34,000
and protection mechanisms that don't interfere with the encounter.

312
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
One doesn't go through life wearing a helmet,

313
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:43,000
but if you're a rock climber or a mountain biker or a skier or a kayaker,

314
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
then it's foolish to not wear that.

315
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:50,000
We get into philosophical discussions about personal freedoms et cetera,

316
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:55,000
but they all kind of go out the door when you see a tragic accident

317
00:20:56,000 --> 00:21:00,000
where someone suffers a head injury because they weren't wearing a helmet.

318
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:07,000
So I am willing to fight those battles in pursuit of safety

319
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:09,000
for all people involved.

320
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:13,000
You know, your average adolescent boy not a real good judge of risk.

321
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:19,000
so it's important to assure their safety and they will push back on occasion.

322
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
I've taught lots of scouts and lots of youths.

323
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:25,000
What you do is you acknowledge the risks,

324
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
you teach them how to take care of themselves and others

325
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:33,000
and in the course of that, you talk always about prevention.

326
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:37,000
So when you teach someone about snake bites,

327
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:41,000
you teach about snake habitat and how to avoid snake bites.

328
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
When you teach about how to treat burns,

329
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:47,000
you talk about fire safety and how to avoid burns.

330
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
When you talk about how to bandage wounds,

331
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:54,000
you talk about how to avoid the situations in which you might get these wounds,

332
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:59,000
either by safe handling of knives, how to avoid blisters, et cetera.

333
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:05,000
So injury prevention should be at least 50% of the educational process.

334
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:07,000
So let me ask you this:

335
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:11,000
What key skills or training would you like to see in a person

336
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:15,000
who's leading a youth group before you would send your own son or daughter out with them?

337
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:20,000
The key skills or knowledge that one would like to see in a leader

338
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:26,000
are those that are particularly relevant to the particular environment

339
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,000
in which they're going to be for one.

340
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:34,000
So, for instance, if it's going to be an experience around bodies of water,

341
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:39,000
I'm going to want the instructor to know water safety particularly well

342
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:43,000
and what to do should someone have a drowning incident.

343
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:51,000
I would also want leaders in scouting groups to have basic, fundamental first aid

344
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:55,000
and health and safety information to treat common disorders

345
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:59,000
that are not necessarily environmental, such as seizures,

346
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:07,000
such as infectious diarrhea, such as headaches, abdominal pain, chest pain,

347
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:12,000
common skin disorders, so that they feel relatively comfortable knowing

348
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:18,000
which kids are sick and which kids are not sick and when to ask for help.

349
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:21,000
You don't have to know exactly how to treat all of these disorders.

350
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:23,000
You need to be able to recognize them. in other words,

351
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:25,000
Yeah, you need to be able to recognize them

352
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:31,000
and you have to have the resources available to either self-help or to get assistance.

353
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:33,000
I mean, that's where the book comes in. for instance,

354
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:37,000
You don't have to memorize the book Medicine for the Outdoors,

355
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:42,000
but you should have thumbed through it to know how to get to the information

356
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:46,000
you need in a hurry, if it's the kind of information that you'll need in a hurry.

357
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:48,000
So it's more important to know what's in the index

358
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:50,000
than it is to have memorized all the content.

359
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:53,000
What I've done is I have a Kindle edition of the book.

360
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:57,000
I can put that on a smartphone and I can put it on an iPod Touch

361
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:01,000
so that it's basically carried with me at all times.

362
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
I'm going to spend enough time to make sure I know how to reference things in it

363
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:07,000
In a situation where those devices might go south on me.

364
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
I would also want to have a hard copy of it.

365
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:17,000
Yeah, that's an issue that we're going to face in publishing. going forward across the entire spectrum.

366
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:22,000
The printed page is fading in terms of popularity

367
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:28,000
and it is much easier to carry a PDA or a Kindle with you.

368
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:33,000
but you have to be able to get to information when the power's off.

369
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:39,000
That's an issue that you decide depending on the environment in which you're going to be.

370
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
If you know you're going to be out for five or six hours

371
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:46,000
and you can protect your devices, then you're in good shape.

372
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:51,000
If you know you're going to be out for a month, then you have to have something written down.

373
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:56,000
What protocol or procedures changed significantly between the fourth and fifth editions of the book?

374
00:24:57,000 --> 00:25:00,000
There weren't a tremendous number of changes.

375
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:03,000
There was actually what I would consider updating.

376
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,000
Any time you read through a book like this,

377
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:10,000
you have to go through all the medications to make sure that there haven't been recalls.

378
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:15,000
You have to check all the antibiotics and bring those things up to date for different disorders,

379
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:16,000
because those things change.

380
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:21,000
Sometimes there are philosophical changes with regard to certain devices.

381
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:29,000
For instance, those of us that instructed people to use the extractor device for snake bite therapy

382
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:33,000
had to do a reversal because there were data coming out

383
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,000
that showed that not only was it not helpful for snake bites,

384
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:39,000
but in certain circumstances it might be harmful.

385
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:46,000
Medicine changes and we do the best we can to keep up with the best knowledge and literature

386
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:50,000
and to write appropriately, but things change.

387
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:55,000
There are things in the fifth edition that I know that I'll change in the sixth edition,

388
00:25:56,000 --> 00:26:00,000
but the good news is none of them are such that there has to be a reprint now.

389
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:04,000
It's just that there will be modifications as techniques improve,

390
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:11,000
as new devices are made available, as we know more about everything that we write about,

391
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:17,000
I notice in the fifth edition too, you've expanded on the idea of making do with what you have

392
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:21,000
in responding to wilderness emergencies. What can you tell me about that?

393
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:25,000
Well, improvisation is one of the not entirely unique

394
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:30,000
but really interesting features of practicing in an austere environment.

395
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:33,000
Whether you're doing global humanitarian relief,

396
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:38,000
whether you're on an expedition with scouts for a couple of weeks in the back country,

397
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:43,000
whether you're just in the park and you have to respond to an emergency,

398
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:47,000
it's really about making do with what's at hand,

399
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:53,000
Without making the book get so big that you don't want to carry it in your backpack.

400
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:59,000
I'm going to continue to try and add improvisational techniques in ways

401
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:07,000
that people will see through to solving problems that they might not otherwise figure out on their own.

402
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:13,000
There was an improvisational technique using dental floss to close a scalp wound that I had never seen before

403
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:18,000
Right, and a way to sew a wound shut without ever stitching the skin

404
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:24,000
by just putting tape on each side, creating a non-sticky edge to the tape

405
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:26,000
and then just sewing the tape strips.

406
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:31,000
Whenever I'm able to discover a technique- and I try all these before they go in the book.

407
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:36,000
What's the career path for somebody who's interested in wilderness medicine?

408
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:40,000
Wilderness medicine is a bona fide specialty of medicine.

409
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:47,000
There are folks who, at the layperson level, are wilderness medicine instructors.

410
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:52,000
I don't know that they've made complete careers out of wilderness medicine.

411
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:58,000
So, for instance, people that are involved in outdoor education programs at the high school and collegiate level

412
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:01,000
and are predominantly educators.

413
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:08,000
On the medical professional side, there are physicians, nurses, EMTs- of all specialties

414
00:28:09,000 --> 00:28:13,000
who practice wilderness medicine by virtue of being expedition physicians,

415
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:18,000
by virtue of practicing in austere environments and needing the skills.

416
00:28:19,000 --> 00:28:25,000
Wilderness EMTs have special training that enable them to be better prepared in those environments.

417
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:28,000
Many of them turn out to be ski patrollers.

418
00:28:29,000 --> 00:28:33,000
Guides of all sorts enhance their education by taking wilderness medicine courses

419
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:36,000
so that they're better able to care for their clients.

420
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:45,000
So, while it's not a traditional career path like being a teacher, a doctor, a lawyer, a chef,

421
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:53,000
it's more of a skill that melds in with whatever a person's primary activity turns out to be.

422
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:57,000
What expeditions or adventures have you got in the future?

423
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:05,000
Well, quite a few. I've been spending more time in the past couple of years doing international medicine,

424
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:12,000
whether that be responding to a disaster situation like Haiti, or doing humanitarian relief

425
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:18,000
or, for instance, we just returned from a month in Nepal helping to teach the first EMT class

426
00:29:19,000 --> 00:29:22,000
that will be engaged in the Nepal ambulance service.

427
00:29:23,000 --> 00:29:30,000
So a lot of my wilderness, outdoor activity is related to professional work that I intend to do in the future.

428
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:32,000
I wish I wasn't working so hard, but I am.

429
00:29:33,000 --> 00:29:38,000
So the amount of time that I can spend purely recreating is diminishing, but that's okay.

430
00:29:39,000 --> 00:29:41,000
I'll get back to that at some point.

431
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:47,000
I'm doing less recreational diving, for instance, and more time underwater,

432
00:29:48,000 --> 00:29:50,000
involved with a research project, for instance,

433
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:57,000
And I try and sneak in a trek in Nepal when I'm working on an ambulance service.

434
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:00,000
So I'm trying to mix business with pleasure as best I can.

435
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:04,000
I really appreciate your work. I love the book. Once again, thanks for taking the time.

436
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:06,000
All right, Take care.

437
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:21,000
I wanted to mention, too, that you can follow Dr Auerbach on Healthlinecom.

438
00:30:22,000 --> 00:30:25,000
He puts a couple of posts up there a week that deal with wilderness medicine,

439
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:31,000
and there will be a link on the blog post that contains this podcast- podcast number 57,

440
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:35,000
so that you can find Dr Auerbach on Healthlinecom.

441
